AB  - Pathogenic fungi deploy the effector-mediated strategies to deal with the host immunity. However, the information for effectors remains largely unknown in entomopathogenic fungi. Trehalose is a dominant carbon source for entomopathogenic fungus to colonize within the host. In this study, a comparative proteomic analysis was used to explore the extracellular proteomes of Beauveria bassiana cultured in a broth with trehalose as a carbon source. The results indicated that ten proteins were specifically observed in the trehalose-based broth, among which a small secreted cysteine-rich protein (named as BbTsp1) was considered as a potential effector. Gene function analysis showed that Bbtsp1 contributed to cell-wall perturbance stress, asexual development, and virulence of B. bassiana. Notably, the protein BbTsp1 was toxic to the insect hosts. A pull-down experiment coupled with mass spectrometry analysis identified the potential interaction proteins of BbTsp1 in the host hemolymph. Further investigation showed that there is a direct interaction between BbTsp1 and the antimicrobial peptide gloverin. A recombinant strain was constructed by overexpressing Bbtsp1 and displayed the enhanced virulence. These findings indicate that BbTsp1 acts as a novel effector to protect the fungal pathogen through direct interaction to deactivate the antimicrobial peptide. This study deepens the understanding of the fungus-host interaction and highlights a new strategy to enhance the biocontrol effic
LA  - eng
UR  - http://europepmc.org/abstract/MED/41461411
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pestbp.2025.106844
