AB  - This study evaluates the lethal and sublethal effects of individual and joint applications of Beauveria bassiana and matrine against Spodoptera litura alongwith examination of transcriptomic responses to these treatments. Both agents significantly impacted S. litura larval emergence (%), and survival of newly emerged larvae (%), with combined applications proving to be more effective than individual treatments. B. bassiana and matrine, whether applied singly or together, extended larval development times, reduced fecundity, lowered pupation rates, pupal weights, and overall life cycle rates. In the F1 generation, combined and individual treatments led to prolonged development and reduced reproductive and population growth rates compared to the control. RNA sequencing revealed differential expression of genes associated with cuticle proteins, zinc finger proteins, vitellogenins, ecdysone-inducible proteins, odorant receptors, and juvenile hormone-binding proteins, shedding light on peroximose, drug metabolism, metabolism of xenobiotics by cytochrome P450, and retinol metabolism pathways affected by the treatments. These findings provide insights into the biochemical and molecular mechanisms underpinning the synergistic effects of B. bassiana and matrine. This research advances knowledge of pest control strategies, emphasizing the potential of integrating entomopathogenic fungi and botanical insecticides for sustainable pest management.
